WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
18,000 square kilometers of Amazon forest stayed standing while soy clearing crossed into the

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:11.760
Cerrado. This is Unintended Consequences, Episode 172. Greenpeace International researchers

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.120
had been following soy from newly cleared Amazon land into European supply chains. In

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.100
April 2006, their report, eating up the Amazon, named Farms and Ports, including Cargill's

00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:28.100
Terminal at Santa Rem, that were taking soy from land cleared in violation of Brazil's

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:34.360
forest code. The report placed Cargill, ADM, Bungee, Dreyfus, and Maggi in the chain that

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:39.780
reached European buyers. Those same companies met that July. They pledged a two-year halt

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:45.680
on buying soy grown on Amazon land cleared after July 24th of that year. The pledge covered

00:00:45.680 --> 00:00:51.360
the Amazon biome only. Later satellite analysis would put the share of new Amazon soy still

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.540
coming from direct forest clearing at roughly 30%.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:59.360
The traders treated a voluntary time-bound rule as a practical way to take the market

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:03.060
reward off new Amazon clearing while they built traceability.

00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:08.680
Public policy already on the books, including the 2004 action plan for the prevention and

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:13.640
control of deforestation, made the private promise look complementary rather than loan.

00:01:14.280 --> 00:01:19.380
They expected slower loss of Amazon habitat without a cut in overall Brazilian soy output.

00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:24.380
But global demand for the crop was still rising, so any workable rule had to leave room

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:26.800
to meet that demand from somewhere inside Brazil.

00:01:27.538 --> 00:01:33.778
The rule took effect at once for the 2006-2007 season and was renewed without an end date

00:01:33.778 --> 00:01:34.738
in 2008.

00:01:35.418 --> 00:01:39.918
Traders asked farmers for global positioning system coordinates and property papers showing

00:01:39.918 --> 00:01:43.078
that Amazon parcels had not been cleared after 2008.

00:01:43.678 --> 00:01:48.738
The share of soy expansion on newly deforested Amazon land fell from about 30 percent in

00:01:48.738 --> 00:01:53.818
the 2001-2006 period to roughly 1% by 2014.

00:01:54.358 --> 00:01:58.498
The company's and environmental groups pointed to that drop across the first decade.

00:01:59.078 --> 00:02:03.558
Skeptics said from the start that the deal stopped at the Amazon biome and left the neighboring

00:02:03.558 --> 00:02:05.318
Cerrado without a matching limit.

00:02:05.718 --> 00:02:10.938
Because enforcement ran through purchase contracts rather than federal law, the traders could watch

00:02:10.938 --> 00:02:14.018
their own suppliers with satellite alerts and farm audits.

00:02:14.478 --> 00:02:17.618
They could not stop planting on land outside the map they had drawn.

00:02:17.618 --> 00:02:23.258
on. Early success rested on a fact of that moment. Most new soy frontiers were still inside

00:02:23.258 --> 00:02:29.469
the Amazon. Once those frontiers moved, the same tools had nothing left to act on. Remote

00:02:29.469 --> 00:02:35.969
sensing work later measured the move. A 2020 paper in Nature Food by researchers at the University

00:02:35.969 --> 00:02:41.769
of California, Santa Barbara and their collaborators used a triple differences model on satellite data.

00:02:42.229 --> 00:02:47.789
They estimated that the moratorium cut annual deforestation rates in soy suitable Amazon locations

00:02:47.789 --> 00:02:50.189
by 0.66 percentage points.

00:02:50.809 --> 00:02:53.749
The same framework set against Serato control areas

00:02:53.749 --> 00:02:56.149
found the effects specific to the Amazon.

00:02:56.729 --> 00:02:59.469
A 2020 study in land use policy,

00:02:59.789 --> 00:03:01.369
modeling land cover change

00:03:01.369 --> 00:03:04.829
showed that after the cutoff, the dynamics of soy expansion

00:03:04.829 --> 00:03:06.309
shifted into the Serato,

00:03:06.769 --> 00:03:10.529
which by then accounted for 54% of Brazil's soybean production.

00:03:11.069 --> 00:03:13.409
From 2007 through 2012,

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:15.909
yearly rates of soy-driven conversion

00:03:15.909 --> 00:03:21.209
of native Serato vegetation stayed between 11 and 23 percent in the states where expansion

00:03:21.209 --> 00:03:22.069
ran fastest.

00:03:22.749 --> 00:03:29.209
A 2019 projection in science advances indicated that, without similar zero conversion commitments,

00:03:29.609 --> 00:03:34.609
continued Serato clearing for soy could reach 10.8 million hectares by 2050.

00:03:35.149 --> 00:03:39.909
The moratorium did not change global soy demand or the relative profit of planting where

00:03:39.909 --> 00:03:40.749
rules were lighter.

00:03:41.449 --> 00:03:44.409
Farmers and traders redirected investment to the savannah.

00:03:44.409 --> 00:03:49.729
Each hectare no longer cleared inside the Amazon state available for planting only if an

00:03:49.729 --> 00:03:54.429
equivalent hectare opened somewhere else, and the Serato offered lower enforcement costs

00:03:54.429 --> 00:03:56.489
and weaker legal limits on conversion.

00:03:57.149 --> 00:04:02.429
Second order effects included continued loss of biodiversity rich Serato habitat and heavier

00:04:02.429 --> 00:04:04.809
pressure on water in the new production zones.

00:04:05.409 --> 00:04:10.269
Third order effects showed up when some Serato municipalities saw rapid infrastructure growth

00:04:10.269 --> 00:04:12.009
tied to the relocated frontier.

00:04:12.009 --> 00:04:15.349
Locking in land use that later proved hard to undo.

00:04:15.969 --> 00:04:19.549
One objection is that Brazilian soy output would have grown anyway.

00:04:19.989 --> 00:04:24.729
The studies isolate the geographic effect by comparing soy suitable zones inside and

00:04:24.729 --> 00:04:29.449
outside the Amazon, and they find the shift larger than the background trend alone would

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:29.749
predict.

00:04:30.389 --> 00:04:34.869
The same traders who had accepted the Amazon boundary kept buying from the new locations,

00:04:35.409 --> 00:04:39.909
so national output did not fall, only the geography of clearing changed.

00:04:39.909 --> 00:04:45.849
The commitment had covered roughly 90% of Amazon soy purchases, and satellite records later

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:51.209
showed the biome itself losing far less soy-linked forest after the cutoff, while the Savannah

00:04:51.209 --> 00:04:54.529
took the redirected planting pressure with no parallel restriction.

00:04:55.461 --> 00:05:01.561
By 2019, calls to extend zero conversion rules to the Serato had produced a proposed Serato

00:05:01.561 --> 00:05:02.621
soy manifesto.

00:05:03.321 --> 00:05:07.741
Major traders did not adopt a binding moratorium comparable to the Amazon version.

00:05:07.741 --> 00:05:14.741
The original Amazon soy moratorium has stayed in force, still watched through satellite and supply chain audits.

00:05:15.401 --> 00:05:24.381
Modeling in science advances suggested that an expanded Serato commitment could avoid an additional 3.6 million hectares of native vegetation loss by 2050.

00:05:25.061 --> 00:05:27.321
No such pact has been implemented at scale.

00:05:28.121 --> 00:05:34.501
Soy expansion now keeps concentrating in the Serato under forest code rules that allow legal clearing on private land.

00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:39.481
Without a parallel rule, the market signal that once rewarded Amazon clearing rewards

00:05:39.481 --> 00:05:44.761
Serato clearing and the infrastructure built for the shifted crop makes reversal more costly.

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:50.360
When the grain traders limited purchases to the Amazon biome, they rewarded planting

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.740
on land that carried no equivalent restriction.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:55.620
Conversion shifted rather than simply shrinking.

00:05:56.180 --> 00:06:01.500
A supply chain rule that defines an acceptable sourcing area without addressing demand or the

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:06.080
economics of the next cheapest region will move production pressure to the unregulated

00:06:06.080 --> 00:06:06.500
zone.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.480
The mechanism is the boundary.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.580
Once the rule stops at a map line, investment follows the line to the side where the rule

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:14.420
does not apply.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.700
Environmental intelligence has been following how a buyer promised drawn around one Brazilian

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:24.360
biome leaves the native vegetation next door exposed to the same market signal.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.900
If a company next year refuses one mapped habitat while demand for the crop is still climbing,

00:06:29.900 --> 00:06:35.660
What would you watch first beyond that line? Land prices, legal clearing, or the roads that

00:06:35.660 --> 00:06:40.180
arrive before anyone writes the next pact? And that's where today's story leaves us.

